  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082348/assets/96a2d22126a8f2575ae97bae8e4a20ef6e7a99fc888b2ce7102c731963d63fea.png)\n\nFind the area bounded between the curve $x={y}^{2}+2y,$ the $y$-axis, and the lines $y=0$ and $y=-2,$ as shown above.",
  "worked_solution": "To find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{c}^{d}x(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{0}({y}^{2}+2y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{0}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y+2{\\int }_{-2}^{0}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}|}_{-2}^{0}+2\\cdot {\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}|}_{-2}^{0} \\\\  & =(0-\\frac{(-2)^{3}}{3})+2(0-\\frac{(-2)^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}-4 \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies to the left of the $y$-axis, we obtained a negative number. So the area that we want is $|-\\frac{4}{3}|=\\frac{4}{3}.$",
